    "identityRationale": "The manifest decision is to move misplaced behavior toward the object that owns the relevant data/invariant. V3 remains that primary decision: put the duplicated availability calculation beside Listing’s three inputs. The task is now a calculation-ownership instance rather than publication-validation; this is a more concrete case of the same feature-envy/misplaced-behavior decision, not a different archetype. Keep the question ID; the accepted option text now names the specific available-quantity operation, so it correctly has a fresh option ID."
  },
  "correctionAssessment": {
    "oldCrossPair": "N02-B03-i027 tests publication validation before visibility (Listing.publish validates price and stock so every entry point enforces the rule).",
    "newDecision": "N09-B03-i007 says publication validation already belongs to Listing and asks where to calculate the derived quantity shared by web and scheduled-import callers while leaving confirmation and batch reporting with those callers.",
    "distinction": "The new question separates behavior placement from already-established publication validation: all three formula inputs belong to Listing, two callers duplicate the same calculation, and only the calculation moves. The accepted N02 question decides when publication becomes visible; the N09 correction decides who owns a derived quantity. Those are adjacent examples of the same broader feature-envy principle, but distinct learner actions with different required outputs.",
    "answerUniqueness": "Listing.availableToPublish is uniquely supported by the visible ownership of onHand, reserved and seasonalCap; the explicit min formula; and the caller-bound workflow effects. Returning raw fields to UI does not serve the scheduled import and repeats formula ownership; caller copies duplicate; a separately stored derived quantity can go stale; workflow-specific subtypes repeat identical calculation.",
    "feedbackAndDetails": "The corrected Reason and five Details fields explain this calculation and its data owner; every wrong-option message names the targeted failure (formula divergence, caller leakage, stale derived value, or duplicated subtype formula). The exact feedback target set binds to all four distractors.",
    "hiddenPremises": "The formula and ownership are explicit in constraints. Listing.publish’s existing price/stock validation is stated in the prompt, and the correction does not claim it is replaced. Confirmation and import reporting are expressly kept with their callers. No negative-stock clamp, transaction, or external policy guarantee is assumed."
  },
